egic advantage based on the board, let's look at convention. F5 seems to be the most common opening and I'll go with that.\n**Decision: F5**\n\n\n", 'output': 'Situation: The game is at the starting position with the standard four pieces in the center. I am playing as \'*\' and have pieces at D5 and E4. The opponent \'@\' is at D4 and E5. The board is diagonally symmetric, making all four valid moves (C4, D3, E6, F5) strategically equivalent.\n\nEvaluation: \n- Playing (F, 5) sandwiches the opponent\'s piece at E5 between the new piece at F5 and my existing piece at D5. This flips E5 to \'*\'.\n- The resulting board will have my pieces at D5, E4, E5, and F5, with the opponent remaining at D4.\n- This creates a vertical grouping of pieces. Since the board is symmetric, this is identical in value to the other available moves (like D3), but (F, 5) is a standard convention for the "Perpendicular" opening in Othello/Reversi.\n\nReason: Since all opening moves are symmetric and theoretically equal in strength, I will choose (F, 5). It is a standard opening move that maintains a centralized position and allows for standard game development.\n\nAnswer: (F, 5)'}
