acent to it, reducing my future mobility.\n4.  **(F, 7)**: This move flips F4, F5, and F6 horizontally. It places a piece adjacent to the opponent\'s edge piece at F8. Crucially, this setup creates a diagonal line (E6-F7) that makes the G8 square (a C-square) a valid move for the opponent. If the opponent plays G8, they are playing into a bad position (C-square), which allows me to capture the H8 corner using my piece at E8 (H8 -> G8 -> F8 -> E8). Even if they avoid G8, playing F7 exerts pressure on the empty bottom-right region without giving up edge or corner access myself.\n5.  **(G, 6)**: Flips G5. However, this gives the opponent easy access to the bottom edge at H6, which I want to control since I own H1.\n\nReason: Move (F, 7) is the best strategic choice. It is a safe move that does not surrender a corner or edge. More importantly, it acts as a trap: it opens up the G8 square for the opponent. Since G8 is a C-square adjacent to the empty H8 corner, forcing or enticing the opponent to play there is a standard winning tactic. If the opponent takes G8, I can capture the H8 corner on my next turn, securing the right edge and significantly increasing my chances of winning.\n\nAnswer: (F, 7)'}
