n:\n1.  **(B, 8)**: This is a very strong move. It places a piece on the edge at B8. Since I already own C8 through H8, and H8 is a corner I control, placing a piece at B8 extends this stable wall. This move flips only one piece (C7), which is excellent strategy (minimizing flips restricts opponent options). It does not immediately allow the opponent to take the corner A8. If the opponent tries to play B7 to set up a line to A8, it will likely be my turn to take A8 first.\n2.  **(C, 1)**: This is also an edge extension (on the left, extending D1-H1 to C1). However, it flips the entire Row C (C2 to C7). Flipping a large number of internal pieces to my color is dangerous here because it provides the opponent with "stepping stones" to play into Row B (e.g., they could play B4 flipping my new piece at C4). This would help the opponent break out of their confinement.\n3.  **(B, 2), (B, 4-7)**: These moves flip various internal pieces or diagonals. They are less valuable than securing a stable edge and generally offer the opponent more counter-play options than the quiet move at B8.\n\nReason:\n(B, 8) is the best choice because it is a stable edge extension connected to my existing corner. It minimizes the number of flipped discs, which keeps the opponent\'s mobility low (they have fewer legal moves to choose from). It avoids the strategic error of (C, 1), which would open up the empty Row B to the opponent.\n\nAnswer: (B, 8)'}
